<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Relationship between token merge index and frequency in training data, indicating they follow a power law.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">RTF-SI evaluates whether it is necessary to include dataset <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> in the training data for constructing the target vocabulary, <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Building on the classic TF-IDF definition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>]</cite>, RTF-SI modifies the normalization used in the TF component <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>. A high RTF-SI for the target dataset <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> suggests that:
❶ Token <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is with high self-information, i.e., it appears infrequently in the training data of the target tokenizer.
❷ Dataset <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> contributes the majority of token <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>’s relative frequency in the underlying distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As a result, the absence of dataset <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> may significantly affect some merged tokens in vocabulary <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>mem</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Power-law fit on token frequency in training data.</figcaption>
