<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Frequency Estimation.</span>  
In practical implementation, an adversary can estimate the RTF component using auxiliary datasets <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{aux}}\leftarrow\mathbb{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>aux</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝔻</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{aux}}\leftarrow\mathbb{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, the SI component is not directly observable, as the frequency <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\left(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\left(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in the training data is not available. To estimate this, we draw on the power law <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, which has been widely applied to approximate word frequency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. According to the power law, when a list of measured values exceeding a threshold <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\textup{min}}\in\mathbb{Z}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\textup{min}}\in\mathbb{Z}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> is sorted in decreasing order, the <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th value is approximately proportional to <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/n^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/n^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.3 Efficient MIA via 𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒⁢𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇 ‣ 4 Attack Methodology ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, there is a power-law relationship between token merge order and frequency. For rigorous verification, we fit the frequency <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\left(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\left(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in a power-law distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})\propto\frac{1}{i^{\alpha}},~\text{where}~t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}~\text{and}~i&gt;x_{\textup{min}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})\propto\frac{1}{i^{\alpha}},~\text{where}~t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}~\text{and}~i&gt;x_{\textup{min}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">The estimation results in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.3 Efficient MIA via 𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒⁢𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇 ‣ 4 Attack Methodology ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> show a small standard error between the estimated and actual values, which supports using the power law to approximate the value <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\left(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\left(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> in the SI component. Given this, RTF-SI can also be computed as:</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<div id="alg2.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l0.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0:</span></span>
              
              
             Target dataset <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, vocabulary of target tokenizer <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math>, underlying distribution <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, sampling times <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, BPE algorithm <math id="alg1.l0.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, power-law fit function <span id="alg1.l0.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">pl.fit</span>, threshold <math id="alg1.l0.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>

