<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">19:</span></span>
              
              
             <math id="alg1.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textsc{Signal}\leftarrow\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-{\mathrm{RTF\text{-}SI}_{\max}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Signal</mtext><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>RTF</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>SI</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textsc{Signal}\leftarrow\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-{\mathrm{RTF\text{-}SI}_{\max}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l19.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg1.l19.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;"> <math id="alg1.l19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="alg1.l19.3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">normalize by sigmoid</em></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">20:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B1B1B1;"># Step 4: Infer the membership</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l21.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">21:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l21.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span>  <math id="alg1.l21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbbm{1}\left[\textsc{Signal}&gt;\tau\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Signal</mtext><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbbm{1}\left[\textsc{Signal}&gt;\tau\right]</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> <span id="alg2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MIA via <math id="alg2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
We train a shadow tokenizer to fit the power-law distribution of token frequency, approximate RTF-SI for each token <math id="alg2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="alg2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;x_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;x_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and compute the membership signal based on the maximum RTF-SI. If the membership signal is larger than a decision-making threshold <math id="alg2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, the dataset <math id="alg2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is inferred as a member. Otherwise, it is inferred as a non-member.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.Thmexample2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmexample2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmexample2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="S4.Thmexample2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RTF-SI under the Power Law</span>)<span id="S4.Thmexample2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmexample2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the power-law distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a><span id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">]</span></cite>, the frequency <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})</annotation></semantics></math> of a token <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">target</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> is proportional to <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/i^{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/i^{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})\propto\frac{1}{i^{\alpha}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})\propto\frac{1}{i^{\alpha}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;x_{\textup{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;x_{\textup{min}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> , <math id="S4.Thmexample2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\textup{min}}\in\mathbb{Z}_{&gt;0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\textup{min}}\in\mathbb{Z}_{&gt;0}</annotation></semantics></math> are constants defined by the power law.
Then, RTF-SI can be approximated by its lower bound:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{RTF\text{-}SI}(D,t_{i},\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})\geq\frac{n_{D}(t_{i})}{\sum_{D^{\prime}\in\mathbb{D}}n_{D^{\prime}}(t_{i})}\cdot\log(\hskip-8.99994pt\sum_{j=x_{\textup{min}}+1}^{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}|}\hskip-8.99994pt{i^{\alpha}}/{j^{\alpha}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>RTF</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>SI</mi></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝔻</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{RTF\text{-}SI}(D,t_{i},\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})\geq\frac{n_{D}(t_{i})}{\sum_{D^{\prime}\in\mathbb{D}}n_{D^{\prime}}(t_{i})}\cdot\log(\hskip-8.99994pt\sum_{j=x_{\textup{min}}+1}^{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}|}\hskip-8.99994pt{i^{\alpha}}/{j^{\alpha}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The detailed proof of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmexample2" title="Theorem 4.2 (RTF-SI under the Power Law). ‣ 4.3 Efficient MIA via 𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒⁢𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇 ‣ 4 Attack Methodology ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. The power law allows an adversary to estimate RTF-SI without directly accessing the frequency <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\left(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\left(t_{i}\mid\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the power law estimates the frequency of tokens <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> with merge index <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;x_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;x_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math>, MIA via <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math> also concentrates on them.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Method.</span>   We outline this attack in four steps below.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I3" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.8em;">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The adversary randomly samples a collection of datasets <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\textup{aux}}\leftarrow\mathbb{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>aux</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝔻</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\textup{aux}}\leftarrow\mathbb{D}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> times, comprising a set <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the adversary trains a shadow tokenizer <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\mathcal{V}_{\text{shadow}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>shadow</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\mathcal{V}_{\text{shadow}}}</annotation></semantics></math> using a <math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\textup{aux}}\subseteq\tilde{\mathbb{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>aux</mtext></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\textup{aux}}\subseteq\tilde{\mathbb{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The adversary fits the power-law distribution in Equation <a href="#S4.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 4.3 Efficient MIA via 𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒⁢𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇 ‣ 4 Attack Methodology ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> using the vocabulary <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\text{shadow}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>shadow</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\text{shadow}}</annotation></semantics></math> and its training data. For each token <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;x_{\text{min}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;x_{\text{min}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the adversary approximate its <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{RTF}(D,t_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>RTF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{RTF}(D,t_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> on set <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{D}}\bigcup\{D\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{D}}\bigcup\{D\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and estimate its <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{SI}(t_{i},\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>SI</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{SI}(t_{i},\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})</annotation></semantics></math> via the fitted power-law distribution.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmexample2" title="Theorem 4.2 (RTF-SI under the Power Law). ‣ 4.3 Efficient MIA via 𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒⁢𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇 ‣ 4 Attack Methodology ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, the membership signal for target dataset <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hskip-20.00003pt\sigma\big(\max_{t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}},\,i&gt;x_{\text{min}}}\underbrace{\frac{n_{D}(t_{i})}{\sum_{D^{\prime}\in\tilde{\mathbb{D}}\bigcup\{D\}}n_{D^{\prime}}(t_{i})}}_{\operatorname{RTF}(D,t_{i})}\cdot\underbrace{\log(\hskip-8.99994pt\sum_{j=x_{\textup{min}}+1}^{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}|}\hskip-8.99994pt{i^{\alpha}}/{j^{\alpha}})}_{\operatorname{SI}(t_{i},\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})}\hskip-1.99997pt\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" rspace="0em" stretchy="true">⋃</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">D</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mstyle></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>RTF</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>i</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>α</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>SI</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hskip-20.00003pt\sigma\big(\max_{t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}},\,i&gt;x_{\text{min}}}\underbrace{\frac{n_{D}(t_{i})}{\sum_{D^{\prime}\in\tilde{\mathbb{D}}\bigcup\{D\}}n_{D^{\prime}}(t_{i})}}_{\operatorname{RTF}(D,t_{i})}\cdot\underbrace{\log(\hskip-8.99994pt\sum_{j=x_{\textup{min}}+1}^{|\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}|}\hskip-8.99994pt{i^{\alpha}}/{j^{\alpha}})}_{\operatorname{SI}(t_{i},\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}})}\hskip-1.99997pt\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sigmoid function <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>. Thereby, the value of Equation <a href="#S4.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Item (iii) ‣ 4.3 Efficient MIA via 𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒⁢𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇 ‣ 4 Attack Methodology ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> ranges from 0 to 1.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iv)</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the membership signal is larger than a decision-making threshold <math id="S4.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, output 1 (<span id="S4.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">member</span>). Otherwise, output 0.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The detailed process of this attack is shown in Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 4.3 Efficient MIA via 𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒⁢𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇 ‣ 4 Attack Methodology ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. The membership signal for MIA via <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the maximum RTF-SI value for the target dataset <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, if including dataset <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> in the training data is necessary for at least one token <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to be merged into the vocabulary <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math>, it suggests that the absence of dataset <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has a significant influence on the already constructed vocabulary. Consequently, it is likely that dataset <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">member</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Attack Evaluation</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first introduce the experimental settings. Next, we develop two shadow-free membership inference methods to serve as additional exploration and baselines. Finally, we present the evaluation results.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets.</span>   According to disclosures from existing LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, the training data for tokenizers is primarily sourced from publicly available web content. To ensure a realistic evaluation of our attacks, we therefore utilize real-world web data from the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">C4</span> corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite> in our evaluation. Specifically, the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">C4</span> corpus is created by cleaning and filtering web pages from Common Crawl, widely used for training and evaluating natural language processing models. In our evaluation, we utilize 1,681,296 web pages across 4,133 websites (i.e., <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}</annotation></semantics></math>) from the <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">C4</span> corpus, with each website treated as a dataset <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05699v1/tokenizer_performance.svg" id="S5.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/159;" width="698" height="159"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>Comparison of tokenizer utility based on the metric of bytes per token. Specifically, “Ours-80000” refers to our trained target tokenizer with a vocabulary size of 80,000 tokens. The above experimental results indicate that the utility performance of the target tokenizers utilized in our evaluations is comparable to that of tokenizers used in state-of-the-art LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05699v1/blind_mia.svg" id="S5.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:558/415;" width="558" height="415"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span>Visualization of the test set using t-SNE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>. The results confirm no distribution shifts in our evaluation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tokenizer Training.</span>   Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>]</cite>, we randomly select half of the datasets in <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}</annotation></semantics></math> to serve as training data. Consistent with DeepSeek <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, we train the target tokenizers using the HuggingFace library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>. The tokenizer vocabulary size ranges from 80,000 to 200,000 tokens, with the upper bound matching that of OpenAI’s latest tokenizer, o200k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>.
For MIA via <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Merge~Similarity}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖾𝗋𝗀𝖾</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖲𝗂𝗆𝗂𝗅𝖺𝗋𝗂𝗍𝗒</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Merge~Similarity}</annotation></semantics></math> and MIA via <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖵𝗈𝖼𝖺𝖻𝗎𝗅𝖺𝗋𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖮𝗏𝖾𝗋𝗅𝖺𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}</annotation></semantics></math>, the adversary trains 96 shadow tokenizers. For MIA via <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math>, the adversary samples the auxiliary datasets 10 times to compose a set <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verification of No Distribution Shifts.</span>   As discussed in previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>, distribution shifts between <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">members</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-members</span> can invalidate the evaluation of MIAs.
In such scenarios, an evaluator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> can exploit bag-of-words features extracted from test samples and train a random forest classifier to detect whether a sample was part of the training data, even without access to the target model.
To ensure that there is no distribution shift in our evaluation, we follow the methodology of prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> by training a random forest classifier and leveraging the bag-of-words features to distinguish between <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">members</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-members</span>. The experimental results are illustrated in Figure <a href="#S5.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Attack Evaluation ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.
The AUC score of 0.513 via this approach to distinguish <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">members</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">non-members</span> confirms the absence of distribution shifts in our evaluations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison to Commercial Tokenizers.</span>   To assess how well our trained target tokenizers in experimental settings mimic the commercial tokenizers in the real world, we compare their utility performance using a standard compression metric: the bytes per token <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. Specifically, following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, we compute the ratio of UTF-8 bytes in a given text to the number of tokens generated by the tokenizer. A higher score is desired. We conduct this evaluation across widely used benchmarks: general English (<span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">WikiText-103</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>), code (<span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GitHub Code</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>), multilingual content (<span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MGSM</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>]</cite>), and mathematics (<span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GPQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite>). As shown in Figure <a href="#S5.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Attack Evaluation ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, the utility performance of our trained tokenizers is comparable to that of the commercial tokenizers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Comparison of MIAs against target tokenizers. Here, BA denotes the metric of balanced accuracy, and TPR refers to the metric of TPR @ 1.0% FPR. The bold values indicate the best performance, while the underlined values denote the second-best. It is observed that MIA via <math id="S5.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖵𝗈𝖼𝖺𝖻𝗎𝗅𝖺𝗋𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖮𝗏𝖾𝗋𝗅𝖺𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}</annotation></semantics></math> and MIA via <math id="S5.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math> outperform other baseline methods.</figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:505.9pt;height:62.5pt;vertical-align:-29.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-190.6pt,23.5pt) scale(0.570317634549881,0.570317634549881) ;">
<table id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
