</span></div>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05699v1/roc_curve_vsize_80000.svg" id="S5.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/164;" width="224" height="164"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F8.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F8.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Vocabulary Size: 80,000</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05699v1/roc_curve_vsize_140000.svg" id="S5.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/164;" width="224" height="164"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F8.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F8.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Vocabulary Size: 140,000</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F8.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.05699v1/roc_curve_vsize_200000.svg" id="S5.F8.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:224/164;" width="224" height="164"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F8.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S5.F8.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Vocabulary Size: 200,000</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Success rate of our attacks on tokenizers with different vocabulary sizes. The experimental results demonstrate that, when evaluated at low false positive rates, both MIA via <math id="S5.F8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖵𝗈𝖼𝖺𝖻𝗎𝗅𝖺𝗋𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖮𝗏𝖾𝗋𝗅𝖺𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}</annotation></semantics></math> and MIA via <math id="S5.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently outperform other methods. Notably, even at a <math id="S5.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.01</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01\%</annotation></semantics></math> false positive rate, both attacks achieve a true positive rate of nearly 10%.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Attack Baselines.</span>   As our work is the first to investigate MIAs targeting tokenizers, there are no existing baselines from prior studies. Therefore, we establish our own baselines by comparing MIA via <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖵𝗈𝖼𝖺𝖻𝗎𝗅𝖺𝗋𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖮𝗏𝖾𝗋𝗅𝖺𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}</annotation></semantics></math> and MIA via <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math> with three other methods: MIA via <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Merge~Similarity}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖬𝖾𝗋𝗀𝖾</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖲𝗂𝗆𝗂𝗅𝖺𝗋𝗂𝗍𝗒</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Merge~Similarity}</annotation></semantics></math> (see Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Baseline: MIA via 𝖬𝖾𝗋𝗀𝖾⁢𝖲𝗂𝗆𝗂𝗅𝖺𝗋𝗂𝗍𝗒 ‣ 4 Attack Methodology ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>), as well as two attack methods we developed. These two attacks are described below:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MIA via <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Naive~Bayes}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖭𝖺𝗂𝗏𝖾</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖡𝖺𝗒𝖾𝗌</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Naive~Bayes}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> 
Since every token originates from at least one of the tokenizer’s training datasets, the adversary can approximate the empirical probability <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(t_{i}\rightarrow D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(t_{i}\rightarrow D)</annotation></semantics></math> that the token <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> comes from the dataset <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, this probability <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(t_{i}\rightarrow D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(t_{i}\rightarrow D)</annotation></semantics></math> can be represented by:</p>
<table id="S5.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(t_{i}\rightarrow D)=\frac{n_{D}(t_{i})}{\sum_{D^{\prime}\in\tilde{\mathbb{D}}\cup\{D\}}n_{D^{\prime}}(t_{i})}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(t_{i}\rightarrow D)=\frac{n_{D}(t_{i})}{\sum_{D^{\prime}\in\tilde{\mathbb{D}}\cup\{D\}}n_{D^{\prime}}(t_{i})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{D}(t_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{D}(t_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the frequency of <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbb{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔻</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbb{D}}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed by sampling auxiliary datasets <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\textup{aux}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>aux</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\textup{aux}}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> times as an estimation for distribution <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔻</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We default to setting <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=10</annotation></semantics></math> in evaluation. The probability that <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>mem</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="S5.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}})=1-\Pr\big(\bigcap_{t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}}{t_{i}\not\rightarrow D}\big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>mem</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⋂</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↛</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}})=1-\Pr\big(\bigcap_{t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}}{t_{i}\not\rightarrow D}\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}\not\rightarrow D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↛</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}\not\rightarrow D</annotation></semantics></math> means <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> was not sourced from <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Assuming rare tokens in training data typically come from disjoint datasets, the probability <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>mem</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}})</annotation></semantics></math> thus can be approximated via the Naive Bayes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Pr(D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>mem</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pr(D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx 1-\prod_{t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\textup{rare}}}\Pr(t_{i}\not\rightarrow D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>rare</mtext></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↛</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx 1-\prod_{t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\textup{rare}}}\Pr(t_{i}\not\rightarrow D)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx 1-\prod_{t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\textup{rare}}}\big(1-\Pr(t_{i}\rightarrow D)\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>rare</mtext></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx 1-\prod_{t_{i}\in\mathcal{V}_{\textup{rare}}}\big(1-\Pr(t_{i}\rightarrow D)\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}_{\textup{rare}}\subseteq\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>rare</mtext></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}_{\textup{rare}}\subseteq\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of tokens with the top <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> merge orders, selected as likely rare tokens in training data (see Equation <a href="#S4.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 4.3 Efficient MIA via 𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒⁢𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇 ‣ 4 Attack Methodology ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). The membership signal for the target dataset <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by the probability <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>mem</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(D\in\mathcal{D}_{\textup{mem}})</annotation></semantics></math>. If it is larger than a threshold <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, output 1 (<span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">member</span>); or, output 0.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MIA via <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Compression~Rate}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖢𝗈𝗆𝗉𝗋𝖾𝗌𝗌𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖱𝖺𝗍𝖾</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Compression~Rate}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> 
The objective of tokenizer training is to maximize the compression rate of a given text corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">109</a>]</cite>.
Based on this optimization objective, we hypothesize that a tokenizer achieves higher compression rates on datasets it was trained on <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.
Leveraging this insight, an adversary can calculate the compression rate of a given dataset <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> using the metric of bytes per token <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>.
The membership signal for the target dataset <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by this compression rate. If it is larger than a decision-making threshold <math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, output 1 (<span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">member</span>); otherwise, output 0.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Metrics.</span>   Following prior studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, we report the MIA performance using three convincing metrics:</p>
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AUC.</span>   This metric quantifies the overall distinguishability of an MIA by computing the area under the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Balanced Accuracy.</span>   This metric (denoted as BA) measures the overall correct predictions on membership by averaging the true positive rate and the true negative rate.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TPR at Low FPR.</span>  
Proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, this metric evaluates the true positive rate (TPR) when the false positive rate (FPR) is low. Following prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, we report TPR @ 1.0% FPR in our evaluations (denoted as TPR).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="145.88" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 700 145.88" width="700"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,145.88) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#808080;--ltx-fill-color:#808080;--ltx-fg-color:#808080;" color="#808080" fill="#808080" fill-opacity="0.5" stroke="#808080" stroke-opacity="0.5"><path style="stroke:none" d="M -3.54 4.13 L -3.54 134.66 C -3.54 138.9 -0.11 142.33 4.13 142.33 L 688.78 142.33 C 693.02 142.33 696.46 138.9 696.46 134.66 L 696.46 4.13 C 696.46 -0.11 693.02 -3.54 688.78 -3.54 L 4.13 -3.54 C -0.11 -3.54 -3.54 -0.11 -3.54 4.13 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 139.97 C 0 143.23 2.64 145.88 5.91 145.88 L 694.09 145.88 C 697.36 145.88 700 143.23 700 139.97 L 700 5.91 C 700 2.64 697.36 0 694.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 139.97 C 1.97 142.14 3.73 143.91 5.91 143.91 L 694.09 143.91 C 696.27 143.91 698.03 142.14 698.03 139.97 L 698.03 5.91 C 698.03 3.73 696.27 1.97 694.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 110.99)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:47.46em;--ltx-fo-height:1.89em;--ltx-fo-depth:7.39em;font-size:10pt;" height="128.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.21)" width="656.71"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:47.46em;">
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Finding 1.<span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">According to prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a><span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">]</span></cite>, scaling up the intelligence of LLMs involves expanding the tokenizer’s vocabulary <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a><span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">]</span></cite> and thus improving its compression efficiency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a><span id="S5.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">]</span></cite>. Yet, the results show it also increases a tokenizer’s vulnerability to MIAs.</span></span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall Performance.</span>  
As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 5 Attack Evaluation ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the MIA via <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖵𝗈𝖼𝖺𝖻𝗎𝗅𝖺𝗋𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖮𝗏𝖾𝗋𝗅𝖺𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}</annotation></semantics></math> and the MIA via <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math> consistently demonstrate strong performance and outperform other baseline methods across different vocabulary sizes. For instance, the MIA via <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖵𝗈𝖼𝖺𝖻𝗎𝗅𝖺𝗋𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖮𝗏𝖾𝗋𝗅𝖺𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves an AUC score of 0.771 when evaluated on a target tokenizer with 200,000 tokens, whereas the MIA via <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves a comparable AUC score of 0.740.
Beyond these results, we observe that the performance of both attacks improves as vocabulary size increases. This trend suggests that MIAs targeting tokenizers in LLMs may become even more effective as state-of-the-art models continue to scale and adopt larger vocabularies in their tokenizers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>. One possible explanation is that larger vocabularies contain more tokens, which may increase the likelihood of merging the distinctive tokens from the training data. As a result, expanding the tokenizer’s vocabulary may unintentionally increase its vulnerability to effective MIAs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Impact of the target dataset size on MIAs. BA denotes balanced accuracy. TPR refers to TPR @ 1.0% FPR. It is observed that MIA via <math id="S5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖵𝗈𝖼𝖺𝖻𝗎𝗅𝖺𝗋𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖮𝗏𝖾𝗋𝗅𝖺𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}</annotation></semantics></math> and MIA via <math id="S5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math> perform better for target datasets with larger sizes.</figcaption>
