<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of Hyperparameter <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>   We further analyze the impact of the hyperparameter <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> on the performance of MIAs. Specifically, in the case of MIA via <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖵𝗈𝖼𝖺𝖻𝗎𝗅𝖺𝗋𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖮𝗏𝖾𝗋𝗅𝖺𝗉</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Vocabulary~Overlap}</annotation></semantics></math>, an adversary trains <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> shadow tokenizers to capture distinctive tokens. As shown in the left plot of Figure <a href="#S5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 5.3 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Attack Evaluation ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, the effectiveness of this attack improves steadily as <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> increases. However, after training more than 112 shadow tokenizers, the performance gain begins to plateau.
In another attack, MIA via <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math>, the adversary samples auxiliary datasets <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> times to estimate probabilities in membership inference. As shown in the right plot of Figure <a href="#S5.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 5.3 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Attack Evaluation ‣ Membership Inference Attacks on Tokenizers of Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, the effectiveness of this attack also increases with larger <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Nevertheless, the performance of MIA via <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖥𝗋𝖾𝗊𝗎𝖾𝗇𝖼𝗒</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝖤𝗌𝗍𝗂𝗆𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Frequency~Estimation}</annotation></semantics></math> tends to be stable once <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds 6. Although the results demonstrate that both attacks benefit from increasing <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, a larger value of <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> also incurs greater resource consumption during inference.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Impact of the target dataset size on defense mechanism (<math id="S5.T8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{min}}=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{min}}=64</annotation></semantics></math>). <math id="S5.T8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}|\leq 80{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>80,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}|\leq 80{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the vocabulary size prior to applying defense is 80,000. TPR refers to TPR @ 1.0% FPR. The results show our MIAs remain effective on large datasets.</figcaption>
