<figure id="A0.T10" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 10: </span>Impact of the target dataset size on defense mechanism (<math id="A0.T10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{min}}=48" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>min</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>48</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{min}}=48</annotation></semantics></math>). <math id="A0.T10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}|\leq 80{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>target</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>80,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{V}_{\text{target}}|\leq 80{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the vocabulary size prior to applying defense is 80,000. TPR refers to TPR @ 1.0% FPR. The results show MIAs remain effective on large datasets.</figcaption>
